A||||8||fiero56||cradle bolts||||4
Z||000000||fiero56||11-21-1999||12:07 PM||||OK, now I might be in trouble.  I'm trying to loosen the bolts that hold the cradle to the frame. I've loosened the passenger side cradle bolt, no problem, it will go clear.  The drivers side bolt turns but so does the nut attcached INSIDE the frame. What do I do? DO I cut a hole in the frame. Then what?  It appears to be a threaded metal sleeve. Lots of rust on the bolt and sleeve.  How do I loosen it?||||reg||4
Z||000001||fiero56||11-21-1999||12:47 PM||||This cradle bolt has halted my engine removal. $#@%!!! My "half hour 'till the eninge is out", has turned into a "I don't know how much longer". Has this ever happened to anyone, or has anyone ever heard of this happening? How was it solved.  I'm stuck here by the computer until I get an answer. Dad's over at the neighbors taking a break until we solve this one. I want it out today.||||reg||8
Z||000002||Tim||11-21-1999||01:20 PM||||Cut the head of the bolt off.<BR>Drop the cradle, see if you can tack weld the nut.  remove what is left of the bolt with heat and pipe whench.  Or cut the nut out from the mount side.  Of course, you need a new bolt and maybe that section of frame from junkyard.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||fiero56||11-21-1999||02:40 PM||||Nope, won't work.  The bolt is still "hidden" under the ledge from the crdale.  No way to get to it to cut it. I think I'm going to end up cutting part of the frame open, get all my stuff done, and take it to a shop to have a "patch" welded on. It's the only solution we've been able to come up with that "should" work. <BR>Had a neighbor who's big into old Pontiacs, he didn't have a solution either.  I e-mailed Ed Parks but no response yet.  I really wanted to get that engine and transmission out today. Maybe next week-end.||||reg||8
Z||000004||Patrick||11-21-1999||03:14 PM||||<BR><B>fiero56</B>, it seems to me that this problem has been discussed here recently. It might be worth your while to start reviewing the posts if you haven't already done so. I think it was within the last month.<P>Just for the heck of it, I've done a search in Technical Discussion & Questions for "cradle bolt" and also for "frame", but I didn't find the thread I was looking for. I remember there was reference to the piece of metal spinning inside of the frame, a hole being cut to access it, and then some debate as to whether or not the hole had to be welded up again. Maybe someone who was involved in that thread could shed some light on the situation.<P>Good luck.||||reg||4
Z||000005||RZURCTN||11-21-1999||05:31 PM||||I had the same problem with mine when I removed the engine.  My remedy?  Keep weight pulling down on the bolt(ie.: the cradle itself).  This will lock the nut down and allow you to remove the cradle bolt.   Be sure to have something underneath the frame to keep it from falling to the floor.  When you return the bolt to the nut, get it started and let the cradle hang from it as you tighten it.  It worked for me anyway.||||reg||1
Z||000006||fiero56||11-21-1999||06:06 PM||||Yeah, I had already tried different weights on it.  Even took all the weight off the jack and let the rear of the cradle rest on that one bolt.  It still didn't work, it just spun.<BR>I ended up cutting a hole in the frame about 2"x2" as Ed Parks advised. After I made the hole, HEAVY duty vice-grips were used to hold the nut in place while I used a 5' long steel bar over the ratchet for leverage. Feels good to win! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000007||theogre||11-21-1999||11:56 PM||||was that a square nut? was it held by a clip or originaly welded to the frame? I have my cradle bushing set but haven't installed it yet. If it's a square nut you might could have made even a smaller hole. There are better tools for square hardware than vice gripes. Smaller too.<P>When you put it back together I'd say just leave the hole you made open. Clean up the edges and coat them with something to prevent rust. A hole that size in that location won't make much diferance. If you have something else break later that requires dropping the craddle you'll only have to cut it open again and that's likely to leave you with an even bigger hole.||||reg||1
Z||000008||fiero56||11-22-1999||08:01 AM||||No, the nut wasn't square, but it did have two flat sides. I tried using a wrench, just slipped over because so much force was required to unbolt it.  I actually had to use TWO pairs of heavy duty vice-grips and a small screwdriver to hold the nut in place while two guys pulled on the 5' "breaker bar"(I have pictures  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">). The nut WAS held in place by a clip that lived under a little welded on housing, but the housing was ripped off all the way around due to the forces put on it by the bar. The bolts on the 88's are a little different because there were no bushings used on them like the '84-'87 models.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 11-22-1999).]||||reg||1
